  "layers": [
    {
      "label": "On the collapsed ceiling",
      "date": "Coin dated 80 CE",
      "observation": "The report places this coin on the ceiling collapse.",
      "inference": "Its position associates it with material above the fallen ceiling. It does not, by itself, date the moment the roof fell. A coin can be lost long after it was minted.",
      "excludes": "“The synagogue was destroyed in 80 CE.” That converts a coin’s date into an event date without establishing the deposition history."
    },
    {
      "label": "The mosaic floor",
      "date": "A building phase",
      "observation": "The mosaic belongs to a sequence of alterations, not the building’s entire lifetime.",
      "inference": "A date for this floor cannot simply be assigned to every wall, earlier room or later use. Archaeologists date contexts and phases before reconstructing a building’s history.",
      "excludes": "“Every feature shown here existed at the same time.” A composite plan can quietly combine successive arrangements."
    },
    {
      "label": "In the mosaic foundation",
      "date": "Coin dated 43 CE",
      "observation": "A coin dated 43 CE was recovered in the reading-room mosaic’s foundation.",
      "inference": "If the context is undisturbed, the floor was laid no earlier than the coin’s issue. Archaeologists call this a terminus post quem: a date after which an event can occur. It is a lower bound, not an exact construction year.",
      "excludes": "“The whole synagogue was founded in 43 CE.” Neither the building’s first use nor the floor’s exact year follows from this coin alone."
    }
  ],
